UNIT, at 8b80c2d2cb183a7374d319d66f17819cf6e99e8b. This gate replaces the earlier one.

npx vitest run --root apps/web: 102 files, 1823 tests, all passing. The suite moved from 1772 to 1823; 51 of those are this task's, 36 from the original /browse work and 15 added now.

WHAT THE NEW TESTS PIN:
  - the route/kind pairing, asserted as a whole map rather than per entry, so a third locked route cannot be added without this test seeing it;
  - THE PATH WINS OVER THE QUERY: readBrowseFilters({kind:"series"}, "movie") yields movie. `/movies?kind=series` is a stale link or somebody poking at the surface, and honouring it would serve series under a path and a heading that both say films;
  - the genre is still read on a locked route, and `/browse` still chooses for itself;
  - buildBrowseHref does NOT repeat a kind the route fixes (`/movies`, `/movies?genre=Drama`) but DOES emit one the route does not fix (`/movies?kind=series`) -- two sources of truth for one filter is a shareable link that can contradict its own path;
  - a locked route's own address round-trips back to the same filters;
  - the filter bar OMITS the kind control on a locked route rather than disabling it, asserted on both `id="browse-kind"` and `name="kind"` so a hidden input cannot creep back in;
  - the form submits to the route it is rendered on, so filtering on /movies does not bounce the viewer to /browse;
  - a selected genre the facets no longer offer is still shown and marked "no titles" -- it happens on exactly the page where it matters, the empty state, whose facet list cannot contain the genre that matched nothing;
  - activeEntryId marks /movies and /tv as themselves and not as browse, because a viewer on /movies is not "in Browse".

THE EXISTING NAV GUARD DID THE REST FOR FREE, and that is worth recording as a return on PL-0756's earlier work: shell-usage.test.ts's "links only to routes that actually exist" resolved both new entries to real page.tsx files without being told about them, and its "every page-like file renders <AppShell>" rule is what kept the shell in the three pages instead of behind the shared component.

RUNTIME, FROM THE REAL PACKAGED PRODUCTION ARTIFACT, because component tests in this suite see only the server-rendered first paint. The desktop target was rebuilt, the sidecar packaged, and the packaged server probed with the token header and a matching Host:

  /                                      200, 15839 bytes, carries skip-link, class="shell", and nav entries Movies, TV Shows, Browse
  /browse                                200, 13658 bytes
  /movies                                200, 13654 bytes, heading "Films"
  /tv                                    200, 13650 bytes, heading "Series"
  /movies?kind=series                    200, 13708 bytes, still "Films" and not "Series"
  /tv?genre=<script>x</script> (encoded) 200, 13783 bytes, no unescaped script tag in the body
  /movies with no token                  403

None of those bodies contains "could not start this page".

ONE HONEST CORRECTION TO MY OWN PROBE, recorded because it is the kind of thing that otherwise reads as a pass. My first run asserted the filter controls were present on all three pages and reported three failures. The controls were correctly ABSENT: this artifact has no catalogue metadata source, so loadBrowseCatalog returns the error state and the surface renders the unavailable panel instead of the filter bar -- which is the documented behaviour ("the filter bar is rendered in every state except the failure one, where there are no facets to offer"). The probe's expectations were wrong, not the code, and the corrected run asserts the heading plus catalog_source_not_configured plus the shell. The populated and filtered states remain covered by the unit tests in a classified process, as before: NODE_ENV is inlined at build time so a production artifact cannot be made to reach them.

NOT CLAIMED: e2e coverage. Playwright cannot be executed in this session. /browse still has no e2e spec and now neither do /movies or /tv; e2e/tests/keyboard-reachability.spec.ts enumerates its routes by hand and does not derive them from PRIMARY_NAVIGATION, so the three are not automatically covered there either. That is named in the report as the next task on this lane rather than left to be discovered.
